{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Vincas Kudirka (31 December [O.S. 19 December] 1858 – 16 November [O.S. 4 November] 1899 ) was a Lithuanian poet and physician, and the author of both the music and lyrics of the Lithuanian National Anthem, "Tautiška giesmė".","title":"Vincas Kudirka"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He is regarded in Lithuania as a National Hero.","title":"Vincas Kudirka"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Kudirka used pen names V. Kapsas, Paežerių Vincas, Vincas Kapsas, P.Vincas, Varpas, Q.D, K., V.K, Perkūnas.","title":"Vincas Kudirka"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Tautiška giesmė ("The National Hymn"; ] ) is the national anthem of Lithuania, also known by its opening words "Lietuva, Tėvyne mūsų" (official translation of the lyrics: "Lithuania, Our Homeland", literally: "Lithuania, Our Fatherland") and as "Lietuvos himnas" (Hymn of Lithuania).","title":"Tautiška giesmė"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The music and lyrics were written in 1898 by Vincas Kudirka, when Lithuania was still part of the Russian Empire.","title":"Tautiška giesmė"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The fifty-word poem was a condensation of Kudirka's conceptions of the Lithuanian state, the Lithuanian people, and their past.","title":"Tautiška giesmė"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Shortly before his death in 1899, the anthem was performed for Lithuanians living in Saint Petersburg, Russia.","title":"Tautiška giesmė"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""How Many Words" is the official second single by the singer-songwriter Blake Lewis, from his debut album "A.D.D. (Audio Day Dream)".","title":"How Many Words"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The song was expected to be followed by "Know My Name", and then "Without You".","title":"How Many Words"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" However, due to being dropped by Arista Records, "How Many Words" is the final single from his debut album.","title":"How Many Words"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He is expected to release a single from his second album at the end of 2009.","title":"How Many Words"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The single was released to mainstream radio format on March 10, 2008.","title":"How Many Words"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Also, an EP featuring remixes of the song was released onto iTunes on May 13, 2008.","title":"How Many Words"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" Lewis performed the song live on the March 6, 2008 results show of the seventh season of "American Idol".","title":"How Many Words"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""O Canada" (French: "Ô Canada" ) is the national anthem of Canada.","title":"O Canada"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The song was originally commissioned by Lieutenant Governor of Quebec Théodore Robitaille for the 1880 Saint-Jean-Baptiste Day ceremony; Calixa Lavallée composed the music, after which, words were written by the poet and judge Sir Adolphe-Basile Routhier.","title":"O Canada"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The lyrics were originally in French; an English version was created in 1906.","title":"O Canada"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Robert Stanley Weir wrote in 1908 another English version, which is the official and most popular version, one that is not a literal translation of the French.","title":"O Canada"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Weir's lyrics have been revised twice, taking their present form in 1980, but the French lyrics remain unaltered.","title":"O Canada"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" "O Canada" had served as a "de facto" national anthem since 1939, officially becoming Canada's national anthem in 1980 when the Act of Parliament making it so received royal assent and became effective on July 1 as part of that year's Dominion Day (now known as Canada Day) celebrations.","title":"O Canada"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":""He Mele Lāhui Hawaiʻ i" ("The Song of the Hawaiian Nation") was composed by Liliuokalani in November 1866 at the request of Kamehameha V, who wanted a national anthem to replace the British anthem "God Save the King".","title":"He Mele Lahui Hawaii"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It replaced Lunalilo's composition "E Ola Ke Aliʻ i Ke Akua" as the national anthem.","title":"He Mele Lahui Hawaii"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Liliʻ uokalani wrote: "The king was present for the purpose of Criticising my new composition of both words and music, and was liberal in his commendations to me on my success.","title":"He Mele Lahui Hawaii"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He admired not only the beauty of music, but spoke enthusiastically of the appropriate words, so well adapted to the air and to the purpose for which they were written.","title":"He Mele Lahui Hawaii"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" This remained in use as our national anthem for some twenty years or more when my brother composed the words "Hawaiʻ i Ponoʻ ī"."","title":"He Mele Lahui Hawaii"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The oldest national anthem, defined as "a song, as of praise, devotion, or patriotism", is the Dutch national anthem "Het Wilhelmus", which was written between 1568 and 1572, but not then given any official status.","title":"List of historical national anthems"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The first anthem to be officially proclaimed as such was "God Save The Queen", adopted by Great Britain in 1745.","title":"List of historical national anthems"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" "Het Wilhelmus" was declared the national anthem of the Netherlands in 1932; both of these anthems remain in use today.","title":"List of historical national anthems"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" A royal or imperial anthem is a song that is similar in patriotic character to a national anthem, but which specifically praises a monarch, or royal dynasty.","title":"List of historical national anthems"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Some states have doubled their royal or imperial anthem as their national anthem.","title":"List of historical national anthems"}],"peer_messages":[" {"sentence_id":0,"text":"\"How Many Words\" is the official second single by the singer-songwriter Blake Lewis, from his debut album \"A.D.D. (Audio Day Dream)\".","title"